module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// The static check: what the compiler can say about a program without running it.
//
// **THE RULE THIS PASS IS BUILT ON: IT MAY ONLY REPORT WHAT THE MACHINE WOULD ALSO REFUSE.** slate is
// dynamically typed and stays that way -- the checker's job is to move a fault from the run that
// finds it to the compile that could have, and never to invent one. So every relation here mirrors
// the run-time test exactly (`fits` against `is_kind`), and anything the walk is not sure of is
// `Any`, which fits everything. A checker that is sometimes wrong is worse than no checker, because
// a program then has to be written around it.
//
// **That is also what makes it gradual rather than optional.** An unannotated parameter is `Any` and
// says nothing; an annotated one says everything it can. TypeScript's bargain, and the reason
// `types.sysl` starts from `Any` rather than from a lattice.
//
// What it checks today: **a call's arity and its arguments**, against a function whose parameters
// were annotated, and a **binary operator** whose two sides cannot meet. What it does not yet check
// is written down at the end of this file.

// -- what fits where -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Whether a value known to be `got` may stand where `want` is asked for.
//
// **`Any` fits in both directions and that is not a hole**, it is the whole of gradual typing: a
// value nothing is known about may go anywhere, and anything may go where nothing is asked. Every
// unknown in the walk becomes `Any`, so this is what keeps the pass from reporting a program it
// merely failed to understand.
fits(got: Type, want: Type) -> bool
    *want.kind match
        Any -> return true
        _ -> ()

    *got.kind match
        Any -> return true

        // **Every alternative must fit, since the value is one of them and nobody knows which.**
        OneOf(alts) ->
            for i in 0..<alts.len()
                if !fits(alts.at(i), want) then return false

            return true
        _ -> ()

    *want.kind match
        // One that accepts is enough on this side.
        OneOf(alts) ->
            for i in 0..<alts.len()
                if fits(got, alts.at(i)) then return true

            false
        NullType -> is_kind_of(got, NullType)
        BooleanType -> is_kind_of(got, BooleanType)
        StringType -> is_kind_of(got, StringType)

        // **An integer does NOT fit a `real`, and that mirrors `is_kind`.** Arithmetic promotes one
        // to the other and a type test does not, so `f(x: real)` given `1` faults at run time --
        // and a checker that let it through would be describing a different language.
        // **A `number` on THIS side fits both, because it may be either.** Refusing it would be
        // refusing a program on the strength of not knowing which one it is, which is the one thing
        // this pass may not do.
        IntegerType -> is_kind_of(got, IntegerType) || is_kind_of(got, NumberType)
        RealType -> is_kind_of(got, RealType) || is_kind_of(got, NumberType)
        NumberType -> is_kind_of(got, IntegerType) || is_kind_of(got, RealType) || is_kind_of(got, NumberType)
        ArrayOf(want_element) -> *got.kind match
            ArrayOf(got_element) -> fits(got_element, want_element)
            _ -> false
        ObjectWith(want_keys, want_values) -> *got.kind match
            // **At least these fields**, which is the object pattern's rule and has to be the type's
            // too, or one annotation would mean two things.
            ObjectWith(got_keys, got_values) ->
                for i in 0..<want_keys.len()
                    var found = false

                    for j in 0..<got_keys.len()
                        if got_keys.at(j) == want_keys.at(i) && fits(got_values.at(j), want_values.at(i))
                            found = true

                    if !found then return false

                true
            _ -> false
        // **A named kind fits its own word and nothing else**, which mirrors `is_kind` exactly: each
        // of them tests one variant of `Value` and no two overlap. There is no promotion here as
        // there is between an integer and a real -- a date is a date.
        NamedType(want_name) -> *got.kind match
            NamedType(got_name) -> got_name == want_name
            _ -> false

        // A regular expression fits a regular expression and nothing else, which mirrors `is_kind`.
        RegexType -> is_kind_of(got, RegexType)
        FunctionType(want_params, _) -> *got.kind match
            // **Arity only, for now.** A parameter's type is contravariant and slate has no syntax
            // for writing a function type down, so every function type the walk builds has `Any`
            // parameters -- a check over them would be a check of `Any` against `Any`.
            FunctionType(got_params, _) -> got_params.len() == want_params.len() || want_params.is_empty()
            _ -> false
        _ -> true
end fits

private is_kind_of(t: Type, k: TypeKind) -> bool
    *t.kind match
        NullType -> k match
            NullType -> true
            _ -> false
        BooleanType -> k match
            BooleanType -> true
            _ -> false
        IntegerType -> k match
            IntegerType -> true
            _ -> false
        RealType -> k match
            RealType -> true
            _ -> false
        NumberType -> k match
            NumberType -> true
            _ -> false
        StringType -> k match
            StringType -> true
            _ -> false
        _ -> false
end is_kind_of

// -- what is known about a name ----------------------------------------------------------------

// The names in scope and what is known about each.
//
// **A flat stack with marks rather than a chain of tables**, because this is compile-time and short:
// a lookup walks backwards to the first match, which is shadowing for free, and leaving a scope is
// one `truncate`. A chain would need a counted box per scope for a structure that never outlives the
// walk.
struct TypeEnv
    names: Buf[string]
    types: Buf[Type]

    // **Whether a name may be written to, which only NARROWING needs.** A `var` reassigned inside
    // the branch that narrowed it makes the narrowed type stale, and a stale type here is too narrow
    // -- which refuses a program that runs. So a mutable name is never narrowed.
    settled: Buf[bool]
    marks: Buf[usize]
end TypeEnv

type_env() -> TypeEnv = TypeEnv(buf(), buf(), buf(), buf())

enter_scope(e: *TypeEnv)
    e.marks.push(e.names.len())

leave_scope(e: *TypeEnv)
    e.marks.pop() match
        Some(mark) ->
            e.names.truncate(mark)
            e.types.truncate(mark)
            e.settled.truncate(mark)
        None -> ()

// **AN OBJECT'S SHAPE IS NOT CARRIED BY A NAME, AND THAT IS THE CONSERVATIVE BOUNDARY OF THIS
// PASS.** An object is mutable and a field may be added to it anywhere -- by the next line, by a
// function it was passed to, through a second name for the same object -- so a shape recorded when
// it was built is a claim that goes out of date without anything local saying so. A stale shape is
// too NARROW, which makes the check refuse a program that runs, and that is the one mistake this
// pass may not make.
//
// **So a shape is checked only where the argument is an object LITERAL written at the call**, which
// cannot have been touched between being built and being handed over. That is the case the mistake
// is actually made in -- `distance(here, { x: 0 })` -- and it costs nothing to be sure about.
// Carrying one through a name wants either immutable records or an escape analysis, and is the next
// piece of work rather than a thing to guess at.
note_type(e: *TypeEnv, name: string, t: Type)
    note_binding(e, name, t, true)

// The same, saying whether the name may later be written to.
note_binding(e: *TypeEnv, name: string, t: Type, settled: bool)
    e.names.push(name)
    e.types.push(unshaped(t))
    e.settled.push(settled)

private unshaped(t: Type) -> Type
    *t.kind match
        ObjectWith(_, _) -> type_of(Any)
        _ -> t

// **Backwards, so the innermost binding of a name is the one found.**
known_type(e: *TypeEnv, name: string) -> Type
    var i = e.names.len()

    loop
        if i == 0 then return type_of(Any)

        i -= 1

        if e.names.at(i) == name then return e.types.at(i)
end known_type

// Whether this environment has heard of a name at all.
//
// **The same walk `known_type` makes, answering presence rather than a type**, which is the question
// an undefined name asks: `known_type` answers `Any` for a name it has never seen and for a name it
// has seen and knows nothing about, and those two are the same answer to a different question.
private has_name(e: *TypeEnv, name: string) -> bool
    var i = e.names.len()

    loop
        if i == 0 then return false

        i -= 1

        if e.names.at(i) == name then return true

// Whether a name is one narrowing may say anything about.
private may_narrow(e: *TypeEnv, name: string) -> bool
    var i = e.names.len()

    loop
        if i == 0 then return false

        i -= 1

        if e.names.at(i) == name then return e.settled.at(i)
end may_narrow

// **`x is string` makes `x` a string for as long as the test held.** The feature a reader coming
// from TypeScript looks for first, and the reason it is safe here is the flag above: a name that may
// be written to is left alone, so a branch that reassigns what it narrowed cannot leave a stale
// claim behind.
//
// **Only a bare name is narrowed.** `o.f is string` would need to say something about `o` that the
// next line could undo by writing to the field, and this pass says nothing about an object's fields
// in the first place.
private narrow_by(cond: Expr, want_true: bool, env: *TypeEnv)
    *cond.kind match
        IsPattern(subject, pat, negated) ->
            // A negated test narrows the OTHER branch, which is the same rule read backwards.
            if negated == want_true then return

            *subject.kind match
                Name(name) ->
                    if !may_narrow(env, name) then return

                    val narrowed = type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0))

                    // A pattern that binds tells us nothing; one that tests tells us everything.
                    first_binding(pat) match
                        Some(_) -> ()
                        None -> note_binding(env, name, narrowed, true)
                _ -> ()

        // **`a && b` narrows by both**, which is what makes `if x is string && len(x) > 0` work --
        // the second operand is already inside the first's narrowing when the machine evaluates it.
        Binary(op, lhs, rhs) ->
            if op == AndAnd && want_true
                narrow_by(lhs, true, env)
                narrow_by(rhs, true, env)
        _ -> ()
end narrow_by

// -- the walk -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Check one file's statements. Reported through the compiler's own channel, so a complaint from here
// stops the program exactly as a parse error does.
check_program(prog: Program)
    var env = type_env()

    // **THE BUILTINS ARE ASKED OF THE INSTALLER RATHER THAN LISTED AGAIN.** An undefined-name check
    // needs to know every name a program starts with, and `install_builtin_types` below carries only
    // the ones whose *type* is worth stating -- `print`, `assert`, `min` and five others deliberately
    // say nothing and would have looked undefined. A second list of 140 names would drift the first
    // time one was added; this builds a real scope with the real installer and asks it.
    builtin_scope = new_env(null)

    install_builtins(builtin_scope)
    enter_scope(&env)
    install_builtin_types(&env)
    check_stmts(prog.stmts, &env)
    leave_scope(&env)
end check_program

// The scope every program starts in, built once per check. **Module storage because the walk reaches
// it from everywhere**, and a root nothing else refers to -- it is thrown away when the next program
// is checked.
var builtin_scope: *EnvObj = null

// Whether a program could find this name at all: something it wrote, or something it was given.
private defined(env: *TypeEnv, name: string) -> bool
    if has_name(env, name) then return true
    if builtin_scope == null then return true

    knows(builtin_scope, name)

// What the builtins take and answer, as far as it can be said without qualification.
//
// **Seeded into the OUTERMOST scope, so that anything a program binds shadows it.** A `val len = ...`,
// a definition, a parameter, an imported name and a pattern binding all go into an inner scope and
// are found first -- which is what keeps this from being a claim about a name the program took for
// itself.
//
// **Only the builtins with a FIXED number of arguments are here.** `print`, `assert`, `min`, `max`,
// `concat`, `sorted`, `sort` and `toJSON` all take a range of counts, and a signature saying one
// number would refuse the other -- so they are left saying nothing, which is what they said before.
//
// **A parameter type is generous and a result type is only given where it is certain.** A wrong
// parameter type refuses a program that runs; a wrong result type does the same one call later, and
// is harder to see. `floor` and `abs` answer an integer for an integer and a real for a real, so
// they say `number`; `sqrt` and `number` are left alone entirely.
private install_builtin_types(e: *TypeEnv)
    val any = type_of(Any)
    val text = type_of(StringType)
    val whole = type_of(IntegerType)
    val truth = type_of(BooleanType)
    val amount = type_of(NumberType)
    val list = type_of(ArrayOf(type_of(Any)))
    val record = type_of(ObjectWith(buf(), buf()))
    val callable = type_of(FunctionType(buf(), type_of(Any)))
    val nothing = type_of(NullType)

    // **A position that may answer `null` says so**, which is a real check rather than a nicety:
    // `indexOf` answers a position or nothing, so handing its answer straight to a parameter that
    // wants a number is a fault the machine would raise, and a union is what lets this see it.
    var maybe_at: Buf[Type] = buf()

    maybe_at.push(whole)
    maybe_at.push(nothing)

    val position = type_of(OneOf(maybe_at))

    // **The temporal builtins, and only the ones whose answer does not depend on what they were
    // given.** `seconds(n)` makes a duration and `seconds(d)` reads one back, so its result is an
    // integer or a duration depending on the argument -- a signature naming one would refuse the
    // other. The same goes for `date`, `time`, `dateTime`, `zone` and the epoch words, and for
    // anything whose arity varies. What is left is the part a program gets wrong most often anyway:
    // the readings.
    val instant_t = type_of(NamedType("instant"))
    val duration_t = type_of(NamedType("duration"))
    val date_t = type_of(NamedType("date"))
    val zone_t = type_of(NamedType("zone"))

    none(e, "now", instant_t)
    none(e, "monotonic", duration_t)
    none(e, "localZone", zone_t)
    one(e, "instant", any, instant_t)
    one(e, "year", any, whole)
    one(e, "month", any, whole)
    one(e, "day", any, whole)
    one(e, "hour", any, whole)
    one(e, "minute", any, whole)
    one(e, "second", any, whole)
    one(e, "dayOfYear", any, whole)
    one(e, "daysInMonth", any, whole)
    one(e, "weekday", any, text)
    one(e, "monthName", any, text)
    one(e, "isLeapYear", any, truth)
    one(e, "parseDate", text, record)
    one(e, "parseTime", text, record)
    one(e, "parseDateTime", text, record)
    one(e, "parseTimestamp", text, record)
    two(e, "format", any, text, text)
    two(e, "startOf", any, text, any)
    two(e, "onOrAfter", date_t, text, date_t)

    // **`env` answers a string or nothing, and that union is a real check**: handing it straight to
    // something that wants a string is a fault the machine would raise, and `?? ""` is what a
    // program writes instead.
    var maybe_text: Buf[Type] = buf()

    maybe_text.push(text)
    maybe_text.push(nothing)

    one(e, "env", text, type_of(OneOf(maybe_text)))

    one(e, "len", any, whole)
    one(e, "chars", text, list)
    one(e, "trim", text, text)
    one(e, "trimStart", text, text)
    one(e, "trimEnd", text, text)
    one(e, "upper", text, text)
    one(e, "lower", text, text)
    one(e, "string", any, text)
    one(e, "boolean", any, truth)
    one(e, "keys", record, list)
    one(e, "values", record, list)
    one(e, "entries", record, list)
    one(e, "parseJSON", text, record)
    one(e, "reversed", list, list)
    one(e, "flat", list, list)
    one(e, "sum", list, amount)
    one(e, "pop", list, any)
    one(e, "clear", list, nothing)
    one(e, "reverse", list, nothing)
    one(e, "abs", amount, amount)
    one(e, "floor", amount, amount)
    one(e, "ceil", amount, amount)
    one(e, "round", amount, amount)
    one(e, "trunc", amount, amount)

    two(e, "split", text, text, list)
    two(e, "join", list, text, text)
    two(e, "startsWith", text, text, truth)
    two(e, "endsWith", text, text, truth)
    two(e, "repeat", text, whole, text)
    two(e, "has", record, any, truth)
    two(e, "contains", any, any, truth)
    two(e, "indexOf", any, any, position)
    two(e, "lastIndexOf", any, any, position)
    two(e, "map", list, callable, list)
    two(e, "filter", list, callable, list)
    two(e, "find", list, callable, any)
    two(e, "findIndex", list, callable, position)
    two(e, "every", list, callable, truth)
    two(e, "some", list, callable, truth)
    two(e, "push", list, any, nothing)
    two(e, "removeAt", list, whole, any)

    three(e, "replace", text, text, text, text)
    three(e, "reduce", list, callable, any, any)
    three(e, "insert", list, whole, any, nothing)
end install_builtin_types

// A builtin of no arguments.
//
// **It is only expressible because the RESULT is known**, `FunctionType(no params, any result)`
// being the bare word `function` and saying nothing. So `now()` can be counted -- `now(5)` is
// refused -- and a builtin of no arguments whose answer nobody can name could not be.
private none(e: *TypeEnv, name: string, result: Type)
    note_type(e, name, type_of(FunctionType(buf(), result)))

private one(e: *TypeEnv, name: string, a: Type, result: Type)
    var params: Buf[Type] = buf()

    params.push(a)
    note_type(e, name, type_of(FunctionType(params, result)))

private two(e: *TypeEnv, name: string, a: Type, b: Type, result: Type)
    var params: Buf[Type] = buf()

    params.push(a)
    params.push(b)
    note_type(e, name, type_of(FunctionType(params, result)))

private three(e: *TypeEnv, name: string, a: Type, b: Type, c: Type, result: Type)
    var params: Buf[Type] = buf()

    params.push(a)
    params.push(b)
    params.push(c)
    note_type(e, name, type_of(FunctionType(params, result)))

// Every name a pattern introduces, as `Any`.
//
// **This is shadowing rather than typing.** A pattern's bindings are not given types yet -- what
// matters here is that a name a pattern took is no longer whatever an outer scope said it was, and
// most of all is no longer a builtin's signature. Without it, `for len in xs` would leave `len`
// looking like the builtin and the next call to it would be counted against the wrong thing.
private note_bindings(pat: Pattern, e: *TypeEnv)
    *pat.kind match
        Bind(name) -> note_type(e, name, type_of(Any))
        At(name, inner) ->
            note_type(e, name, type_of(Any))
            note_bindings(inner, e)
        ArrayPat(items, _, rest) ->
            for i in 0..<items.len()
                note_bindings(items.at(i), e)

            rest match
                Some(name) -> note_type(e, name, type_of(Any))
                None -> ()
        ObjectPat(_, values) ->
            for i in 0..<values.len()
                note_bindings(values.at(i), e)
        OrPat(alts) ->
            for i in 0..<alts.len()
                note_bindings(alts.at(i), e)
        _ -> ()
end note_bindings

// **Every definition in a block is noted before any statement is walked**, so a function may call
// one written below it and one written above may call it back. Whether the *machine* has run the
// definition by then is a different question and a different fault.
private check_stmts(stmts: Buf[Stmt], env: *TypeEnv)
    for i in 0..<stmts.len()
        *stmts.at(i).kind match
            Def(name, params, body, is_async) -> note_type(env, name, signature_of(params, body_at(body), is_async))

            // **EVERY name this block binds is noted before any body is walked, not only the
            // definitions.** A closure may name something declared later in the block it sits in --
            //
            //     val probe = { equals: o -> clear(xs) }
            //     val xs = [probe]
            //
            // -- which runs, because the body runs after `xs` exists. Checking that body against the
            // scope as it stood where the closure was *written* would refuse it, and refusing a
            // program that runs is the one thing this pass may not do. The type is `Any` here and the
            // real one is noted in the walk below, which shadows this by being nearer.
            Val(name, _) -> note_type(env, name, type_of(Any))
            Var(name, _) -> note_binding(env, name, type_of(Any), false)
            Unpack(pat, _, _) -> note_bindings(pat, env)
            ImportNames(_, _, _, _, locals) ->
                for j in 0..<locals.len()
                    note_type(env, locals.at(j), type_of(Any))
            ImportAll(_, _, _, alias) -> note_type(env, alias, type_of(Any))
            _ -> ()

    for i in 0..<stmts.len()
        check_stmt(stmts.at(i), env)
end check_stmts

// The type of a definition, read back off the checks its annotations were desugared into.
//
// **The annotations are not on the `Def` and must not be put there.** The parser turns `f(x: string)`
// into a `CheckType` statement at the top of the body and `-> string` into an `Ensures` around it,
// which is what keeps every node downstream of a definition free of them. So this reads them back,
// and a parameter with no `CheckType` is `Any` -- which is what an unannotated parameter means.
private signature_of(params: Buf[string], body: Expr, is_async: bool) -> Type
    var types: Buf[Type] = buf()

    for i in 0..<params.len()
        types.push(annotation_for(params.at(i), body))

    // **An `async` function answers a promise and not what its body says**, so a result annotation
    // on one describes the awaited value rather than the call. Nothing here can say that yet, so it
    // says nothing.
    val result = if is_async then type_of(Any) else result_annotation(body)

    type_of(FunctionType(types, result))
end signature_of

// The `CheckType` a parameter's annotation became, if it had one.
private annotation_for(name: string, body: Expr) -> Type
    *body.kind match
        Ensures(inner, _, _) -> return annotation_for(name, inner)
        Block(stmts) ->
            for i in 0..<stmts.len()
                *stmts.at(i).kind match
                    // **Only the run of checks at the TOP of the body is read.** They are what the
                    // parser put there; a `CheckType` further down could not exist, and stopping at
                    // the first ordinary statement is what keeps that true if one ever could.
                    CheckType(checked, _, pat) ->
                        if checked == name then return type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0))
                    _ -> return type_of(Any)

            type_of(Any)
        _ -> type_of(Any)
end annotation_for

private result_annotation(body: Expr) -> Type
    *body.kind match
        Ensures(_, _, pat) -> type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0))
        _ -> type_of(Any)

private check_stmt(s: Stmt, env: *TypeEnv)
    *s.kind match
        Val(name, init) ->
            check_expr(init, env)
            note_type(env, name, type_of_expr(init, env))

        // **A `var` is `Any` whatever it was initialised with**, because a later assignment may put
        // anything there and this pass does not follow assignments. Narrowing one would be reporting
        // a program the checker had not read all of.
        Var(name, init) ->
            check_expr(init, env)
            note_binding(env, name, type_of(Any), false)
        Unpack(pat, init, _) ->
            check_expr(init, env)
            note_bindings(pat, env)
        Assign(_, target, value) ->
            check_expr(target, env)
            check_expr(value, env)
        MultiAssign(_, targets, values) ->
            for i in 0..<targets.len()
                check_expr(targets.at(i), env)

            for i in 0..<values.len()
                check_expr(values.at(i), env)
        Def(_, params, body, _) -> check_body(params, body_at(body), env)

        // **An imported name is `Any` and, more to the point, is no longer a builtin's signature.**
        // A file that imports its own `map` would otherwise be checked against slate's.
        ImportNames(_, _, _, _, locals) ->
            for i in 0..<locals.len()
                note_type(env, locals.at(i), type_of(Any))
        ImportAll(_, _, _, alias) -> note_type(env, alias, type_of(Any))
        Eval(x) -> check_expr(x, env)
        Return(value) -> value match
            Some(x) -> check_expr(x, env)
            None -> ()
        _ -> ()
end check_stmt

// A definition's body, with its parameters in scope at the types its annotations gave them.
private check_body(params: Buf[string], body: Expr, env: *TypeEnv)
    enter_scope(env)

    for i in 0..<params.len()
        note_type(env, params.at(i), annotation_for(params.at(i), body))

    check_expr(body, env)
    leave_scope(env)
end check_body

// -- what an expression is -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The type of an expression, as far as the pass can tell. **`Any` wherever it cannot.**
type_of_expr(x: Expr, env: *TypeEnv) -> Type
    *x.kind match
        IntLit(_) -> type_of(IntegerType)
        RealLit(_) -> type_of(RealType)
        StrLit(_) -> type_of(StringType)
        BoolLit(_) -> type_of(BooleanType)
        Interp(_) -> type_of(StringType)
        Name(name) -> known_type(env, name)
        ArrayLit(_) -> type_of(ArrayOf(type_of(Any)))
        ArrayRepeat(_, _) -> type_of(ArrayOf(type_of(Any)))
        ObjectLit(keys, _, values) ->
            var out: Buf[Type] = buf()

            for i in 0..<values.len()
                out.push(type_of_expr(values.at(i), env))

            type_of(ObjectWith(keys, out))
        Lambda(params, _, _) ->
            var out: Buf[Type] = buf()

            for i in 0..<params.len()
                out.push(type_of(Any))

            type_of(FunctionType(out, type_of(Any)))

        // A comparison and an `is` are booleans whatever they compared.
        Compare(_, _) -> type_of(BooleanType)
        IsPattern(_, _, _) -> type_of(BooleanType)
        Unary(op, rhs) -> op match
            Bang -> type_of(BooleanType)
            _ -> type_of_expr(rhs, env)
        Binary(op, lhs, rhs) -> binary_type(op, type_of_expr(lhs, env), type_of_expr(rhs, env))
        Call(callee, _) -> *type_of_expr(callee, env).kind match
            FunctionType(_, result) -> result
            _ -> type_of(Any)
        Ensures(_, _, pat) -> type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0))
        _ -> type_of(Any)
end type_of_expr

// What an operator answers, given what it was handed.
//
// **Only the cases that are certain.** `+` over two integers is an integer; over anything less known
// it is `Any`, because the operator is overloaded on strings and promotes across the two number
// kinds. Saying `number` where it could be a string would be a claim the checker cannot support.
private binary_type(op: Tok, lhs: Type, rhs: Type) -> Type
    op match
        AndAnd -> return type_of(BooleanType)
        OrOr -> return type_of(BooleanType)
        _ -> ()

    if !is_arithmetic(op) then return type_of(Any)

    if is_kind_of(lhs, StringType) && is_kind_of(rhs, StringType) && op == Plus
        return type_of(StringType)

    if is_kind_of(lhs, IntegerType) && is_kind_of(rhs, IntegerType) then return type_of(IntegerType)

    // **A real anywhere in it makes the answer a real**, which is slate's promotion. Saying
    // `number` here instead would be true and too weak: `1.0 * 2` would then not fit a `real`
    // parameter, and the checker would be refusing a program that runs.
    if numeric(lhs) && numeric(rhs)
        if is_kind_of(lhs, RealType) || is_kind_of(rhs, RealType) then return type_of(RealType)

        return type_of(NumberType)

    type_of(Any)
end binary_type

private numeric(t: Type) -> bool =
    is_kind_of(t, IntegerType) || is_kind_of(t, RealType) || is_kind_of(t, NumberType)

private is_arithmetic(op: Tok) -> bool = op match
    Plus -> true
    Minus -> true
    Star -> true
    Slash -> true
    Percent -> true
    _ -> false

// -- what is complained about ----------------------------------------------------------------------

private check_expr(x: Expr, env: *TypeEnv)
    val at = x.span

    *x.kind match
        Call(callee, args) ->
            check_expr(callee, env)

            for i in 0..<args.len()
                check_expr(args.at(i), env)

            check_method(callee, env)
            check_call(callee, args, env, at)
        Binary(op, lhs, rhs) ->
            check_expr(lhs, env)
            check_expr(rhs, env)
            check_binary(op, lhs, rhs, env, at)
        Unary(_, rhs) -> check_expr(rhs, env)
        Block(stmts) ->
            enter_scope(env)
            check_stmts(stmts, env)
            leave_scope(env)
        ArrayLit(items) -> for i in 0..<items.len() do check_expr(items.at(i), env)
        ObjectLit(_, _, values) -> for i in 0..<values.len() do check_expr(values.at(i), env)
        Interp(pieces) -> for i in 0..<pieces.len() do check_expr(pieces.at(i), env)
        Compare(operands, _) -> for i in 0..<operands.len() do check_expr(operands.at(i), env)
        Field(target, _, _) -> check_expr(target, env)
        Index(target, key) ->
            check_expr(target, env)
            check_expr(key, env)
        WithFields(base, _, values) ->
            check_expr(base, env)

            for i in 0..<values.len()
                check_expr(values.at(i), env)
        Await(inner) -> check_expr(inner, env)
        Ensures(value, declared, pat) ->
            check_expr(value, env)

            // **A body that is one expression is checked against what the definition promised.** A
            // block answers `Any` here and is left alone, which is also what keeps an early `return`
            // out of it: nothing about a block's value is known, so nothing is claimed.
            val answered = type_of_expr(value, env)
            val promised = type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0))

            if !fits(answered, promised)
                complain_here(value.span, s"this was declared to answer ${declared}, and answers ${show_type(answered)}")
        IfExpr(cond, then_branch, else_branch) ->
            check_expr(cond, env)

            enter_scope(env)
            narrow_by(cond, true, env)
            check_expr(then_branch, env)
            leave_scope(env)

            else_branch match
                Some(other) ->
                    enter_scope(env)
                    narrow_by(cond, false, env)
                    check_expr(other, env)
                    leave_scope(env)
                None -> ()
        MatchExpr(subject, arms) ->
            check_expr(subject, env)

            for i in 0..<arms.len()
                // **A pattern's bindings are not typed yet**, so the arm's body is walked in a scope
                // that knows nothing new -- which is `Any` for every name the pattern introduced, and
                // therefore silent about all of them.
                enter_scope(env)
                note_bindings(arms.at(i).pattern, env)
                narrow_by_arm(subject, arms.at(i).pattern, env)
                check_expr(arms.at(i).body, env)
                leave_scope(env)
        While(_, cond, body, otherwise) ->
            check_expr(cond, env)
            check_expr(body, env)
            check_otherwise(otherwise, env)
        For(_, binding, iter, body, otherwise) ->
            check_expr(iter, env)
            enter_scope(env)
            note_bindings(binding, env)
            check_expr(body, env)
            leave_scope(env)
            check_otherwise(otherwise, env)
        Loop(_, body) -> check_expr(body, env)
        DoWhile(_, body, cond, otherwise) ->
            check_expr(body, env)
            check_expr(cond, env)
            check_otherwise(otherwise, env)
        TryCatch(body, caught, handler) ->
            check_expr(body, env)
            enter_scope(env)
            note_type(env, caught, type_of(Any))
            check_expr(handler, env)
            leave_scope(env)
        Lambda(params, body, _) -> check_body(params, body_at(body), env)

        // **A NAME NOTHING HAS BOUND IS REFUSED BEFORE IT RUNS**, which is the most common mistake
        // there is in any language and the one a dynamic language usually finds last -- a typo on a
        // branch nobody took until a customer took it.
        //
        // **It is sound because the machine would refuse the same program**: a name is bound by a
        // `val`, a `var`, a definition, a parameter, a pattern, an import or the builtins, and this
        // pass tracks every one of those. A name used before the `val` that binds it in the same
        // block faults at run time too -- the scope has nothing in it yet -- so refusing it early is
        // the same answer sooner.
        Name(name) ->
            if !defined(env, name)
                complain_here(x.span, s"`${name}` is not defined${nearest(env, name)}")

        // **An `is` BINDS INTO THE SURROUNDING SCOPE**, which is the machine's rule -- `if x is
        // { n }` leaves `n` standing after it. Nothing here checked the subject or noted the
        // bindings before, which was harmless while every unknown name was `Any` and is not once a
        // name nothing bound is a complaint.
        IsPattern(subject, pat, _) ->
            check_expr(subject, env)
            note_bindings(pat, env)
        _ -> ()
end check_expr

// The nearest name the program does know, where one is close enough to be worth naming.
//
// **A suggestion is only offered where it is nearly right.** "Did you mean" is the most annoying
// thing a compiler does when it guesses badly, so the distance has to be one edit for a short name
// and two for a long one, and nothing else is offered at all.
private nearest(env: *TypeEnv, name: string) -> string
    var best = ""
    var best_at: usize = 3

    for i in 0..<env.names.len()
        val d = distance(name, env.names.at(i))

        if d < best_at
            best = env.names.at(i)
            best_at = d

    val limit: usize = if name.bytes.len <= 4 then 1 else 2

    if best == "" || best_at > limit then "" else s" -- did you mean `${best}`?"

// How many single-character edits apart two names are, up to the point where nobody cares.
private distance(a: string, b: string) -> usize
    val x = a.bytes
    val y = b.bytes

    if x.len == 0 then return y.len
    if y.len == 0 then return x.len

    var previous: Buf[usize] = buf()
    var current: Buf[usize] = buf()

    for j in 0..<(y.len + 1)
        previous.push(j)
        current.push(0)

    for i in 0..<x.len
        current.set(0, i + 1)

        for j in 0..<y.len
            val substitute = previous.at(j) + (if x[i] == y[j] then 0 else 1)
            val insert = current.at(j) + 1
            val remove = previous.at(j + 1) + 1
            var least = substitute

            if insert < least then least = insert
            if remove < least then least = remove

            current.set(j + 1, least)

        for j in 0..<(y.len + 1)
            previous.set(j, current.at(j))

    previous.at(y.len)
end distance

// **An arm narrows its subject the way an `is` narrows its operand**, and for the same reason: the
// arm only runs where the pattern took. `note_bindings` has already put the arm's own names in, so
// this is on top of them and a name the pattern bound wins by being nearer.
private narrow_by_arm(subject: Expr, pat: Pattern, env: *TypeEnv)
    *subject.kind match
        Name(name) ->
            if !may_narrow(env, name) then return

            first_binding(pat) match
                Some(_) -> ()
                None -> note_binding(env, name, type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0)), true)
        _ -> ()
end narrow_by_arm

private check_otherwise(otherwise: Option[Expr], env: *TypeEnv)
    otherwise match
        Some(x) -> check_expr(x, env)
        None -> ()

// `x.m(...)` where the kind of `x` is known: a method name that kind does not have.
//
// **This is the most common mistake a checker can catch in a dynamic language** -- a name off by a
// letter, or one borrowed from another type -- and it is sound here because it asks the machine's own
// question. `methods_of` is the table `builtin_method` dispatches through, so what this refuses is
// exactly what the machine would refuse, and a name added to a kind is added in one place.
//
// **An object is not asked about**, its names being the program's; nor is anything whose type is not
// certain, which is the ordinary case in unannotated code.
private check_method(callee: Expr, env: *TypeEnv)
    *callee.kind match
        Field(target, name, _) ->
            val t = type_of_expr(target, env)

            if !certain(t) then return

            val kind = method_kind_word(t)

            if kind == "" then return
            if methods_of(kind, name).is_some() then return

            // **The same sentence the machine would have printed**, down to the article: a reader
            // who meets this at compile time and then at run time must not have to work out that the
            // two are one complaint.
            complain_here(callee.span, s"`${name}` is not something ${article(kind)} ${kind} can do")
        _ -> ()
end check_method

// The word a type's methods are looked up under, or `""` where it has none to look up.
//
// **Only the kinds `method.sysl` has a table for**, which is what keeps this from claiming anything
// about an object, a function or `null`: an object's names belong to the program, and the other two
// have no methods at all, so a complaint about either would have to come from somewhere that knows
// more than this does.
private method_kind_word(t: Type) -> string
    *t.kind match
        StringType -> "string"

        // **`integer` and `real` rather than `number`**, since that is what `type_name` says at run
        // time and the two messages have to read alike. `methods_of` takes all three words and
        // answers from the one table.
        IntegerType -> "integer"
        RealType -> "real"
        NumberType -> "number"
        ArrayOf(_) -> "array"
        NamedType(name) -> name
        RegexType -> "regex"
        _ -> ""
end method_kind_word

// `a` or `an`, so that a complaint reads as English.
private article(word: string) -> string
    val b = word.bytes

    if b.len == 0 then return "a"

    val c = b[0]

    if c == u8('a') || c == u8('e') || c == u8('i') || c == u8('o') || c == u8('u') then "an" else "a"
end article

// A call whose callee is a function the pass knows the shape of.
//
// **Arity first, and it stops there when it is wrong**, because a mismatched count makes every
// argument's position a guess -- reporting four type errors for one missing argument is the
// commonest way a checker becomes something to work around.
private check_call(callee: Expr, args: Buf[Expr], env: *TypeEnv, at: Span)
    // **A spread makes the count unknowable, so this pass says nothing about the call at all.**
    // `f(...xs)` gives `f` as many arguments as `xs` has elements, which is a run-time fact; a
    // checker counting the spread as one argument would refuse a program that runs, and that is the
    // one mistake it may not make. The machine still counts, at the call.
    for i in 0..<args.len()
        *args.at(i).kind match
            Spread(_) -> return
            _ -> ()

    *type_of_expr(callee, env).kind match
        FunctionType(params, result) ->
            // A lambda's type has `Any` parameters and says nothing; a definition with no annotated
            // parameter has the same shape. Either way there is nothing here to check against.
            // **`FunctionType(no params, any result)` IS THE BARE WORD `function`**, which says
            // callable and nothing about arity -- `f: function` writes exactly that. Counting
            // against it would refuse `f(1)` inside a function whose parameter was annotated
            // `function`, which runs. The cost is that a genuine `() -> x` has the same shape and
            // goes uncounted too, and the cost is worth paying to be sure.
            if !says_nothing(params, result) && params.len() != args.len()
                complain_here(at, s"${named(callee)} takes ${params.len()} arguments and this gives it ${args.len()}")
                return
            end if

            // **Nothing is checked where the counts disagree**, which is also the case a type
            // saying nothing about arity leaves behind: there is no parameter opposite an argument
            // to compare it to.
            if params.len() != args.len() then return

            for i in 0..<args.len()
                val want = params.at(i)
                val got = type_of_expr(args.at(i), env)

                if !fits(got, want)
                    complain_here(args.at(i).span, s"${named(callee)} takes ${show_type(want)} here, and this is ${show_type(got)}")
        _ -> ()
end check_call

private says_nothing(params: Buf[Type], result: Type) -> bool
    if !params.is_empty() then return false

    *result.kind match
        Any -> true
        _ -> false
end says_nothing

// **Only where BOTH sides are known and neither could meet the other.** `1 + "a"` is a fault the
// machine would raise, so saying it now is moving the same complaint earlier; anything less certain
// is left alone.
private check_binary(op: Tok, lhs: Expr, rhs: Expr, env: *TypeEnv, at: Span)
    if !is_arithmetic(op) then return

    val a = type_of_expr(lhs, env)
    val b = type_of_expr(rhs, env)

    if numeric(a) && numeric(b) then return
    if op == Plus && is_kind_of(a, StringType) && is_kind_of(b, StringType) then return

    // **A temporal operand is left to the machine, and this pass may not do otherwise.** What `+`,
    // `-` and `*` mean over the eight temporal kinds is a table of its own in `time.sysl` -- an
    // instant minus an instant is a duration, a zoned reading plus a period is a zoned reading, a
    // duration times an integer is a duration -- and restating it here would be a second copy to
    // keep in step by hand. It refused `monotonic() - a`, which runs, the first time it was tried.
    if is_named_kind(a) || is_named_kind(b) then return

    // Anything not certainly one of the two workable pairings is left to the machine.
    if !certain(a) || !certain(b) then return

    complain_here(at, s"`${op_text(op)}` does not apply to ${show_type(a)} and ${show_type(b)}")
end check_binary

// Whether this is one of the kinds that is nothing but its word. Their operators are their own
// subject, and a generator has none at all.
private is_named_kind(t: Type) -> bool
    *t.kind match
        NamedType(_) -> true
        _ -> false

// Whether enough is known about a type to argue from it.
private certain(t: Type) -> bool
    *t.kind match
        Any -> false
        OneOf(_) -> false
        _ -> true
end certain

// How a callee is named in a complaint, which is its name where it has one.
private named(callee: Expr) -> string
    *callee.kind match
        Name(name) -> s"`${name}`"
        Field(_, name, _) -> s"`${name}`"
        _ -> "this function"
end named

// -- what this pass does NOT do yet ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Written down because the absences are decisions rather than oversights, and because each one is
// the next piece of work rather than a thing to be surprised by.
//
// - **A name bound by a PATTERN has no type.** A `match` arm, a `catch`, a destructuring binding and
//   a `for` head all introduce names the walk leaves as `Any`. The patterns already carry the
//   information -- `type_from_pattern` would answer for most of them -- and threading it in is the
//   obvious next step.
// - **A `var` is always `Any`**, because this pass does not follow assignments. Narrowing one would
//   be reporting a program the checker has not read all of.
// - **A field read is `Any`**, even off an object whose type is known. The information is there in
//   `ObjectWith`; what is missing is only the lookup.
// - **A function's RESULT is not checked against its body.** `-> integer` is enforced by
//   `CheckResult` at run time and believed here.
// - **Narrowing reaches an `if` and a `match` arm and nothing else.** A `while`, a guard and the
//   right-hand side of `||` all leave the subject as it was.
// - **A temporal operator is not checked at all.** What `+`, `-` and `*` mean over the eight
//   temporal kinds is a table in `time.sysl`, and restating it here would be a second copy kept in
//   step by hand -- so `check_binary` steps aside for them. Reading that table rather than copying
//   it is the way in, if it is ever worth doing.
//
// **A METHOD NAME IS CHECKED NOW, AND IT IS THE MOST USEFUL THING THIS PASS DOES.** `"abc".push(1)`
// and `date(2026, 1, 1).hour()` are refused before they run, which is the commonest mistake there is
// in a language with per-kind method tables. It is sound because it asks the machine's own question:
// `methods_of` in `method.sysl` is the table `builtin_method` dispatches through, so a name added to
// a kind is added in one place and the two can never disagree. An object is never asked about -- its
// names are the program's -- and nor is a receiver whose type is not certain.
